*
how will you resolve bean dependency amiguity  or how can we handle multiple bean of same type  

-> suppose the two class implements same interface 

public interface MyService {}

@Component
public class ServiceA implements MyService {}

@Component
public class ServiceB implements MyService {}

@Service
public class ConsumerService {

    private final MyService myService;

    @Autowired
    public ConsumerService(MyService myService) {
        this.myService = myService;
    }
}

Error: NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException because Spring finds two MyService beans: ServiceA and ServiceB.
// here it is not able to define from which beans the method to call
-->ans-
1.
Use @Primary (Recommended for default bean)

Mark one bean as primary:
@Component
@Primary
public class ServiceA implements MyService {}

@Component
public class ServiceB implements MyService {}

Now Spring injects ServiceA by default.

✅ Simple and clean
⚠️ Only one default can be primary

2.
Use @Qualifier (Recommended for explicit injection)

Specify exact bean name:

@Component("serviceA")
public class ServiceA implements MyService {}

@Component("serviceB")
public class ServiceB implements MyService {}

@Service
public class ConsumerService {

    private final MyService myService;

    @Autowired
    public ConsumerService(@Qualifier("serviceB") MyService myService) {
        this.myService = myService;
    }
}

✔ Explicit
✔ Works well when multiple beans are required in different places

3.
using @Resource

@Component("serviceA")
public class ServiceA implements MyService {}

@Component("serviceB")
public class ServiceB implements MyService {}

@Service
public class ConsumerService {

    @Resource(name = "serviceB")
    private MyService myService;

    public void execute() {
        myService.performTask();
    }
}
How it works:

name = "serviceB" → Spring looks for a bean with that exact name
Injects ServiceB into myService
⚠️ Field cannot be final (because Spring sets it via reflection)


