what is bean scope and diff type of bean scope
-->
Bean scope defines the lifecycle and visibility of a Spring bean — i.e., how many instances Spring will create and how long they live.
By default, all Spring beans are singleton.


| Scope       | Instance per          | Lifecycle                                     | Typical Use            |
| ----------- | --------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| singleton   | Spring context        | Startup → Shutdown                            | Services, Repositories |
| prototype   | Each request for bean | New instance created each time                | Temp objects, tasks    |
| request     | HTTP request          | Starts with request, destroyed after          | Request data holder    |
| session     | HTTP session          | Starts with session, destroyed on session end | User-specific data     |
| application | Servlet context       | App-wide                                      | Shared resources       |
| websocket   | WebSocket session     | Starts with WS session                        | WS-specific data       |


Bean scope defines the lifecycle and visibility of a Spring bean. The main scopes are:
singleton: default, one instance per Spring context
prototype: new instance every injection
request: one intance per HTTP request
session: one instance per HTTP session
application: one instance per ServletContext
websocket: one instance per WebSocket session

Examples

4.1 Singleton (Default)
@Component
public class MySingletonService {}

@Autowired
private MySingletonService service1;

@Autowired
private MySingletonService service2;

service1 == service2 // true, same instance

4.2 Prototype
2
@Scope("prototype")
public class MyPrototypeService {}

@Autowired
private MyPrototypeService service1;

@Autowired
private MyPrototypeService service2;

service1 == service2 // false, different instances

4.3 Request Scope (Web Only)
@Component
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class MyRequestBean {}


Spring creates one instance per HTTP request
proxyMode is required for injection into singleton beans

4.4 Session Scope (Web Only)
@Component
@Scope(value = "session", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class MySessionBean {}

One instance per user session
Useful for storing session-specific data


WHY proxyMode is used here?
-->
In Spring, proxyMode is used because of how scoped beans (like request/session) are injected into other beans.
Let’s break it down clearly.

The problem proxyMode solves
@Scope("request") means:

A new bean instance is created for each HTTP request
But most beans that depend on it (like @Service, @Component) are:
Singletons (created once at startup)
👉 A singleton cannot directly hold a request-scoped bean, because:

At startup, there is no HTTP request yet
Spring wouldn’t know which request’s instance to inject

What proxyMode does
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)

This tells Spring:
“Don’t inject the real request-scoped object.
Inject a proxy instead.”

That proxy:
Is a placeholder
Looks like the real object
Delegates calls to the correct request-specific instance at runtime

So:
The singleton bean holds the proxy
During an HTTP request, the proxy forwards calls to the correct request-scoped bean

Why TARGET_CLASS?
ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS

Means:
Spring creates a class-based proxy (using CGLIB)
Works even if the class does not implement an interface

Other option:
ScopedProxyMode.INTERFACES


Example scenario
@Service
public class MyService {
    private final RequestBean requestBean;

    public MyService(RequestBean requestBean) {
        this.requestBean = requestBean;
    }
}

@Component
@Scope(value = "request", proxyMode = ScopedProxyMode.TARGET_CLASS)
public class RequestBean {
}


What happens:
MyService is a singleton
RequestBean is request-scoped
Spring injects a proxy of RequestBean
Each HTTP request gets its own real RequestBean instance

Without proxyMode
You would get an error like:
Scope 'request' is not active for the current thread

Because Spring tries to create the bean when no request exists.

Summary

proxyMode is needed to safely inject request/session-scoped beans into singletons
It works by injecting a proxy instead of the real object
TARGET_CLASS creates a class-based proxy (most common choice)



*
can we inject prototype bean in singleton bean ? if yes what happen
-->
if we inject prototype bean in singleton bean it behaves as singleton

Yes, it’s allowed
⚠️ But by default, only one prototype instance is created, not a new one each time you use it.

Singleton bean: created once by Spring
Prototype bean: supposed to create a new instance every time it’s requested

But when you do this:
@Component
public class SingletonBean {

    @Autowired
    private PrototypeBean prototypeBean;
}

Why this happens
Dependency injection happens only once, when the singleton is created
Spring does not re-inject dependencies every time you call a method


How to get a new prototype instance each time

If you truly need a new prototype bean every time, use one of these approaches:
✅ Option 1: ObjectProvider (recommended)     //object provider is spring utility to obtain beans lazily
@Component
public class SingletonBean {

    @Autowired
    private ObjectProvider<PrototypeBean> prototypeProvider;

    public void usePrototype() {
        PrototypeBean prototype = prototypeProvider.getObject();      //Every call to getObject() → new prototype instance created
    }
}
✔ A new PrototypeBean is created each time getObject() is called


Option 2: @Lookup
@Component
public class SingletonBean {

    @Lookup
    public PrototypeBean getPrototypeBean() {
        return null;
    }
}
✔ Spring overrides this method to return a new prototype instance each call


